The problem is the git repo doesn't have the actual bundled files needed for Windows. Here's what I found:

Git out/vs/workbench/workbench.desktop.main.js = 19 KB (source map, not the real bundle)
Working app needs workbench.desktop.main.js = ~20 MB (compiled/bundled)
Git out/main.js = 68 KB (source file)
Working app needs main.js = ~1 MB (bundled)
The Mac app works because it was built with the full build toolchain (gulp vscode-darwin-arm64-min or similar) which produces the proper bundles. The git only has individual source files — not the compiled bundles.

What you need to do on the Mac:

Run the cross-compilation build targeting Windows:


cd /path/to/volt
gulp vscode-win32-x64-min
This will produce a folder like VSCode-win32-x64/ (or similar path in .build/) with:

Bundled out/main.js (~1MB)
Bundled out/vs/workbench/workbench.desktop.main.js (~20MB)
Bundled out/vs/workbench/workbench.desktop.main.css
Proper package.json
product.json with correct checksums
node_modules.asar
The Plaxio.exe binary (Electron)
Transfer that entire folder to this Windows machine (zip it, upload to Drive/GitHub releases, etc.), and I'll package it into an installer with Inno Setup + rcedit for the icon. That's all that's needed — the Inno Setup part takes 30 seconds once we have the clean build folder.
